-- Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab) Chapter 1, Periodic Table and Periodic Properties: 59 original MCQs, CHE-0001 to
-- CHE-0059. Each has the bank's template (stem, four options, key, why the key is right, why each other option is wrong)
-- and its source page in the textbook. Reviewed and approved for the bank by the site owner, so they go straight to live.
--
-- Banks (lib/banks.ts): MDCAT through the question's own chapter, "Periodic table and periodicity" (Chemistry), which NUMS and
-- AKU reuse; FSc Part 1 through bank_questions, as Chemistry "Periodic table and periodic properties".
-- Full mocks pick from every live question of a subject, so these join them automatically. They are not in the free set.
--
-- The writer is the site owner's admin account; if it doesn't exist (a fresh local database) nothing is inserted.
-- INSERT OR IGNORE keeps the file safe to run twice and never overwrites an edited copy.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Chemistry', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-0001', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Historical background: Döbereiner''s triads', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 2 (§1.1)', 'In a Döbereiner triad, chlorine (atomic mass 35.5) and iodine (atomic mass 127) are the first and third members. What atomic mass would Döbereiner''s rule predict for the middle member, bromine?', '["63.5", "81.3", "91.5", "162.5"]', 1, 'Döbereiner noticed that the atomic mass of the middle element of a triad is roughly the average of the other two: (35.5 + 127) ÷ 2 ≈ 81.3. Bromine''s actual atomic mass, 79.90, is close to this.', '{"0": "63.5 is half of iodine''s mass alone. The rule averages the first and third members.", "2": "91.5 is the difference between the two masses (127 − 35.5), not their average.", "3": "162.5 is the sum of the two masses. It still has to be halved."}'),
  ('CHE-0002', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Historical background: Mendeleev', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 2 (§1.1)', 'What was the main reason Mendeleev''s periodic table was accepted as a major success?', '["He arranged the elements in order of increasing atomic number.", "He determined atomic numbers using X-ray emission.", "He left gaps for undiscovered elements and correctly predicted their properties.", "He placed the noble gases in a separate group at the right of the table."]', 2, 'Mendeleev arranged the elements by increasing atomic mass, left gaps for elements not yet discovered and predicted their atomic masses and properties. These predictions proved accurate when the elements were found.', '{"0": "Mendeleev ordered elements by atomic mass. Ordering by atomic number came later, from Moseley''s work.", "1": "Finding atomic numbers from X-ray emission was Moseley''s work in 1913.", "3": "The book credits Mendeleev''s success to his gaps and predictions, not to the placement of the noble gases."}'),
  ('CHE-0003', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Historical background: Moseley', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 2 (§1.1)', 'Moseley established the exact atomic numbers of the known elements in 1913. Which technique did he use?', '["X-ray emission", "Alpha-particle scattering", "Mass spectrometry", "Cathode-ray deflection"]', 0, 'Moseley used X-ray emission to find the atomic numbers of elements. He then arranged the elements by atomic number instead of atomic mass, which removed the discrepancies in Mendeleev''s table.', '{"1": "Alpha-particle scattering was Rutherford''s experiment that revealed the nucleus, not how atomic numbers were measured.", "2": "Mass spectrometry measures the masses of atoms and isotopes, not atomic numbers.", "3": "Cathode-ray deflection was used to study the electron, not to find atomic numbers."}'),
  ('CHE-0004', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Modern periodic law', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 2 (§1.1) and p. 6 (§1.7); masses from Fig. 1.1, p. 3', 'In the modern periodic table argon (average atomic mass 39.95) is placed before potassium (average atomic mass 39.10). Which statement best justifies this order?', '["Argon is a gas, and gases are always placed before metals.", "Potassium''s atomic mass was measured incorrectly in Mendeleev''s time.", "Elements are placed in order of increasing atomic mass only within each block.", "Elements are arranged by atomic number, and argon (18) comes before potassium (19)."]', 3, 'The modern periodic law says properties are periodic functions of atomic number, not atomic mass. Argon''s atomic number is 18 and potassium''s is 19, so argon comes first even though it is heavier. This keeps argon with the noble gases and potassium with the alkali metals.', '{"0": "There is no rule that gases come before metals. Hydrogen, a gas, sits above the alkali metals, for example.", "1": "Potassium''s mass of 39.10 is correct. The order is by atomic number, not mass.", "2": "The modern table orders all elements by atomic number, not by atomic mass within blocks."}'),
  ('CHE-0005', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Modern periodic table: groups', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 3 (§1.2)', 'Elements in the same group of the modern periodic table show similar chemical properties mainly because they have the same', '["number of electron shells.", "number of valence electrons.", "number of neutrons.", "atomic mass."]', 1, 'Elements in a group have the same number of valence electrons, so they react in similar ways.', '{"0": "The number of shells increases down a group. It is elements in the same period that share a number of shells.", "2": "Neutron number differs between elements of a group (and even between isotopes) and does not decide chemical behaviour.", "3": "Atomic mass increases down a group and does not decide chemical properties."}'),
  ('CHE-0006', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Metals, non-metals and metalloids', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 3 (§1.3)', 'Which of the following elements is a metalloid?', '["Aluminium", "Phosphorus", "Germanium", "Tin"]', 2, 'The stair-step line runs from boron down to polonium, and the metalloids along it include Si, Ge, As, Sb and Te. Germanium is one of them.', '{"0": "Aluminium lies to the left of the stair-step line, so it is a metal.", "1": "Phosphorus lies to the right of the line, so it is a non-metal.", "3": "Tin lies to the left of the line (next to antimony), so it is a metal."}'),
  ('CHE-0007', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Metals, non-metals and metalloids', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 3 (§1.3)', 'Which element is the exception to the rule that elements to the left of the stair-step line are metals?', '["Lithium", "Helium", "Boron", "Hydrogen"]', 3, 'Hydrogen is placed at the top of the far-left column but is a non-metal. The book names it as the exception.', '{"0": "Lithium is a metal (an alkali metal), so it follows the rule.", "1": "Helium is at the far right of the table, with the noble gases.", "2": "Boron is where the stair-step line begins; it is a metalloid, not an element on the far left."}'),
  ('CHE-0008', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Blocks in the periodic table', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 4 (§1.4)', 'An atom has the electronic configuration 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁶ 4s². To which block of the periodic table does it belong?', '["s-block", "p-block", "d-block", "f-block"]', 2, 'Elements are placed in blocks by the subshell that holds their valence (last-filled) electrons. Here the 3d subshell is being filled, so the element (iron) is a transition element of the d-block.', '{"0": "The 4s subshell is full, but the electrons being added are in 3d. s-block elements are only groups 1 and 2.", "1": "The p subshells here are completely filled inner subshells, not the subshell being filled.", "3": "There are no f electrons in this configuration. f-block elements are the lanthanides and actinides."}'),
  ('CHE-0009', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Blocks in the periodic table', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 4 (§1.4); Fig. 1.1, p. 3', 'Uranium (Z = 92) is an actinide. In which block is it placed?', '["f-block", "d-block", "p-block", "s-block"]', 0, 'The two series at the bottom of the table, the lanthanides and the actinides, are f-block elements. Uranium is in the actinide series (89 to 103).', '{"1": "The d-block holds the transition elements in groups 3 to 12, not the actinides.", "2": "The p-block is groups 13 to 18.", "3": "The s-block is groups 1 and 2."}'),
  ('CHE-0010', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Families: alkali metals', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 4 (§1.5); basic oxides p. 15', 'Which statement about the alkali metals is correct?', '["They have two valence electrons and are less reactive than Group 2 metals.", "They have one valence electron and are the most reactive metals.", "They have one valence electron and form acidic oxides.", "They are named so because they are mostly found in the earth''s crust."]', 1, 'Group 1 metals (Li, Na, K, Rb, Cs, Fr) have one valence electron and are the most reactive metals. They are called alkali metals because they form alkalis with water.', '{"0": "Two valence electrons and lower reactivity describe the alkaline earth metals of Group 2.", "2": "Metals form basic oxides, such as Na₂O, which give alkalis with water.", "3": "Being found in the earth is the reason for the name of the alkaline earth metals, not the alkali metals."}'),
  ('CHE-0011', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Families: transition metals', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 4 (§1.5)', 'Which property is NOT characteristic of transition metals such as Cr, Fe, Co and Cu?', '["They show variable oxidation states.", "They mostly form coloured compounds.", "They occupy the middle of the periodic table.", "They show only one fixed oxidation state."]', 3, 'Transition metals show variable oxidation states, so a single fixed oxidation state is not one of their properties.', '{"0": "Variable oxidation states are a typical property of transition metals.", "1": "Transition metals mostly form coloured compounds.", "2": "The transition metals make up the largest family, in the middle of the table."}'),
  ('CHE-0012', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Families: chalcogens', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 5 (§1.5)', 'The Group 16 elements are called chalcogens because', '["most ores of copper are oxides or sulfides.", "they all form salts directly with alkali metals.", "they are all non-metals with six valence electrons.", "they were first isolated from chalk."]', 0, 'Chalcogen comes from the Greek chalkos (copper), because most copper ores are oxides or sulfides.', '{"1": "Forming salts with metals is the meaning of halogen (salt-former), for Group 17.", "2": "Group 16 is not all non-metals: the book notes livermorium is a metal.", "3": "The name has nothing to do with chalk."}'),
  ('CHE-0013', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Families: halogens', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 5 (§1.5)', 'The word "halogen" means', '["salt-former.", "sea-element.", "colour-giver.", "acid-former."]', 0, 'Halogens (Group 17) react readily with alkali metals and alkaline earth metals to form salts, so the name means salt-former.', '{"1": "Halogens are found in sea salt, but the name means salt-former, not sea-element.", "2": "Many halogens are coloured, but that is not the meaning of the name.", "3": "Halogens do form acids such as HCl, but the name means salt-former."}'),
  ('CHE-0014', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Families: noble gases', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 5 (§1.5 and Keep in Mind box)', 'The preparation of xenon compounds such as XePtF₆ led chemists to', '["move xenon to Group 17.", "classify xenon as a metalloid.", "replace the term \"inert gases\" with \"noble gases\".", "conclude that all Group 18 elements are highly reactive."]', 2, 'Group 18 elements are almost unreactive, but compounds of xenon such as xenon hexafluoroplatinate showed they are not completely inert. So the name inert gases was changed to noble gases.', '{"0": "Xenon stays in Group 18 because of its complete outer shell.", "1": "Xenon is a noble gas, not a metalloid.", "3": "Group 18 elements are still almost unreactive under normal conditions and rarely form compounds."}'),
  ('CHE-0015', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Position and electronic configuration', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, pp. 5 to 6 (§1.6)', 'An element is in period 3 and group 15. What is its valence-shell configuration?', '["2s² 2p³", "3s² 3p⁵", "3s² 3p¹", "3s² 3p³"]', 3, 'The period number gives the valence shell, n = 3. A group 15 element has 5 valence electrons (just as group 13 has 3), which fill as 3s² 3p³. The element is phosphorus.', '{"0": "2s² 2p³ has its valence electrons in shell 2, so it is a period 2 element (nitrogen).", "1": "3s² 3p⁵ has 7 valence electrons, so it is in group 17 (chlorine).", "2": "3s² 3p¹ has 3 valence electrons, so it is in group 13 (aluminium)."}'),
  ('CHE-0016', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Position and electronic configuration', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, pp. 5 to 6 (§1.6); p. 3 (§1.2)', 'An element has the configuration 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁵. Its position in the periodic table is', '["period 3, group 17.", "period 3, group 7.", "period 2, group 17.", "period 5, group 17."]', 0, 'The outermost shell is n = 3, so the element is in period 3. It has 7 valence electrons (3s² 3p⁵) in the p-block, which is group 17 in the 18-group table. The element is chlorine.', '{"1": "Group 7 is the old A-group style of numbering. In the modern 18-group table this is group 17.", "2": "Period 2 elements have their valence electrons in shell 2. Here they are in shell 3.", "3": "5 is the number of 3p electrons, not the number of shells."}'),
  ('CHE-0017', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Position and electronic configuration', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 5 (§1.6); Fig. 1.1, p. 3', 'An element lies in period 4 and the s-block and has two valence electrons. The element is', '["magnesium.", "calcium.", "strontium.", "titanium."]', 1, 'Period 4 means the valence electrons are in shell 4, and s-block with two valence electrons means 4s². This is calcium, in group 2 and period 4.', '{"0": "Magnesium (3s²) is in period 3.", "2": "Strontium (5s²) is in period 5.", "3": "Titanium is a d-block (transition) element."}'),
  ('CHE-0018', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Atomic radius', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 6 (§1.7.1)', 'The distance between the nuclei of the two atoms in a Cl₂ molecule is 198 pm. The atomic radius of chlorine is', '["49.5 pm", "99 pm", "198 pm", "396 pm"]', 1, 'Atomic radius is half the distance between two identical atoms bonded together: 198 ÷ 2 = 99 pm.', '{"0": "49.5 pm is a quarter of the distance. The radius is half of it.", "2": "198 pm is the whole distance between the two nuclei, which is two radii.", "3": "396 pm doubles the distance instead of halving it."}'),
  ('CHE-0019', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Atomic radius', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, pp. 6 to 7 (§1.7.1)', 'Atomic radius increases down a group even though nuclear charge also increases. The best explanation is that', '["the number of valence electrons increases down the group.", "effective nuclear charge increases sharply down the group.", "new electron shells are added and shielding increases, which outweighs the greater nuclear charge.", "atoms lower in a group form only ionic bonds."]', 2, 'Going down a group, new shells are added and the inner electrons shield the outer ones more. This outweighs the increase in nuclear charge, so the atom gets larger.', '{"0": "Elements in the same group have the same number of valence electrons.", "1": "A sharp rise in effective nuclear charge would pull electrons in and make atoms smaller, not larger.", "3": "The type of bond an atom forms does not explain the trend in atomic radius."}'),
  ('CHE-0020', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Atomic radius', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 6 (§1.7.1, Fig. 1.3)', 'Which of the following period 3 atoms has the largest atomic radius?', '["Na", "Mg", "P", "Cl"]', 0, 'Across a period the number of shells stays the same while nuclear charge increases and pulls the electron cloud closer. So atomic radius decreases from left to right, and sodium, at the left, is largest.', '{"1": "Magnesium has one more proton than sodium in the same number of shells, so it is smaller.", "2": "Phosphorus is further right in period 3, so it is smaller than sodium.", "3": "Chlorine is near the right end of period 3, so it is among the smallest atoms in the period."}'),
  ('CHE-0021', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Ionic radius', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 7 (§1.7.2)', 'A sodium ion, Na⁺, is smaller than a sodium atom because', '["Na⁺ gains a proton when it forms.", "Na⁺ loses its outermost shell and the nucleus pulls the remaining electrons more strongly.", "electron repulsion increases when Na⁺ forms.", "the nuclear charge of Na⁺ is larger than that of Na."]', 1, 'When sodium loses its single 3s electron it loses a whole shell. The same nuclear charge now acts on fewer electrons and pulls them closer, so the ion is smaller.', '{"0": "Forming an ion changes the number of electrons, not protons.", "2": "Removing an electron lowers repulsion between electrons. Increased repulsion is why anions are larger.", "3": "Na and Na⁺ have the same nuclear charge, +11."}'),
  ('CHE-0022', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Ionic radius', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 7 (§1.7.2)', 'The chloride ion, Cl⁻, is larger than the chlorine atom. Which statement explains this?', '["The added electron increases electron–electron repulsion, so the electron cloud expands.", "The added electron goes into a new shell.", "The nuclear charge decreases when an electron is added.", "Chlorine loses a proton when it forms Cl⁻."]', 0, 'Adding an electron increases the repulsion between electrons, so the electron cloud spreads out and the anion is larger than the atom.', '{"1": "The extra electron goes into the existing 3p subshell (3p⁵ becomes 3p⁶), not a new shell.", "2": "The nuclear charge stays +17. Only the number of electrons changes.", "3": "Ions form by gaining or losing electrons. The number of protons does not change."}'),
  ('CHE-0023', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Ionic radius', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 7 (§1.7.2, Fig. 1.4)', 'Which of these ions has the largest ionic radius?', '["Be²⁺", "Mg²⁺", "Ca²⁺", "Sr²⁺"]', 3, 'These are Group 2 ions with the same charge. Ionic radius increases down a group because each step adds a shell, so Sr²⁺, the lowest of the four, is largest.', '{"0": "Be²⁺ is at the top of Group 2, so it is the smallest of these ions.", "1": "Mg²⁺ has fewer shells than Ca²⁺ and Sr²⁺, so it is smaller.", "2": "Ca²⁺ is above strontium in the group, so it has one shell fewer than Sr²⁺."}'),
  ('CHE-0024', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Ionic radius', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 7 (§1.7.2, Fig. 1.4)', 'Which order of ionic radius is correct?', '["F⁻ > O²⁻ > N³⁻", "O²⁻ > N³⁻ > F⁻", "N³⁻ > O²⁻ > F⁻", "N³⁻ > F⁻ > O²⁻"]', 2, 'Across a period, anion radius decreases because nuclear charge increases and pulls the electrons closer. N (Z = 7), O (Z = 8) and F (Z = 9) are in period 2, so N³⁻ is largest and F⁻ smallest. All three ions have 10 electrons, so the one with the fewest protons is largest.', '{"0": "This is the reverse order. F⁻ has the most protons for the same 10 electrons, so it is the smallest.", "1": "N³⁻ has fewer protons than O²⁻ for the same number of electrons, so it is larger than O²⁻.", "3": "F⁻ has more protons than O²⁻, so it is smaller than O²⁻, not larger."}'),
  ('CHE-0025', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Ionization energy: definition', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 8 (§1.7.3)', 'Which equation represents the first ionization energy of magnesium?', '["Mg(s) → Mg⁺(g) + e⁻", "Mg(g) → Mg²⁺(g) + 2e⁻", "Mg(g) + e⁻ → Mg⁻(g)", "Mg(g) → Mg⁺(g) + e⁻"]', 3, 'First ionization energy is the energy needed to remove one electron from each atom in one mole of gaseous atoms, forming one mole of gaseous 1+ ions.', '{"0": "The atoms must start in the gaseous state, not as a solid.", "1": "This removes two electrons; it is the sum of the first and second ionization energies.", "2": "Adding an electron to a gaseous atom describes electron affinity."}'),
  ('CHE-0026', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Ionization energy: definition', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 8 (§1.7.3)', 'How many successive ionization energies does a calcium atom (Z = 20) have?', '["2", "4", "20", "40"]', 2, 'An element has as many ionization energies as its atomic number, because each of its electrons can be removed in turn. Calcium has 20 electrons, so it has 20 ionization energies.', '{"0": "2 is the number of calcium''s valence electrons, not the total number of electrons that can be removed.", "1": "4 is the number of shells calcium has, not the number of electrons.", "3": "40 is calcium''s approximate atomic mass, not its number of electrons."}'),
  ('CHE-0027', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Ionization energy: calculation', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 8 (§1.7.3)', 'The first ionization energy of sodium is 494 kJ mol⁻¹. How much energy is needed to convert 0.5 mol of gaseous sodium atoms into gaseous Na⁺ ions?', '["247 kJ", "494 kJ", "988 kJ", "124 kJ"]', 0, '494 kJ is needed per mole of gaseous sodium atoms, so 0.5 mol needs 0.5 × 494 = 247 kJ.', '{"1": "494 kJ is the energy for a whole mole, not half a mole.", "2": "988 kJ is the energy for 2 mol, doubling instead of halving.", "3": "124 kJ is the energy for about 0.25 mol."}'),
  ('CHE-0028', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Successive ionization energies', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 8 (§1.7.3); p. 5 (§1.6)', 'The first three ionization energies of calcium are 590, 1150 and 4940 kJ mol⁻¹. The very large jump to the third value occurs because', '["the third electron is removed from a half-filled subshell.", "calcium has three valence electrons.", "the third electron is removed from a 4p orbital.", "after two electrons are lost, the third must come from a stable, filled inner shell closer to the nucleus."]', 3, 'Calcium is in group 2, so it has only two valence electrons (4s²). After both are removed, Ca²⁺ has a stable, completely filled noble-gas arrangement. The third electron must come from this inner shell, which is closer to the nucleus and less shielded, so much more energy is needed.', '{"0": "Ca²⁺ has completely filled subshells (3p⁶), not a half-filled one.", "1": "Calcium is in group 2, so it has two valence electrons. The jump comes after the second.", "2": "Calcium has no electrons in 4p. The third electron comes from 3p."}'),
  ('CHE-0029', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Ionization energy: electronic arrangement', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 8 (§1.7.3)', 'Nitrogen has a higher first ionization energy than oxygen, even though oxygen has the greater nuclear charge. Why?', '["Nitrogen''s 2p subshell is half-filled, which is especially stable.", "Oxygen has more electron shells than nitrogen.", "Nitrogen is smaller than oxygen.", "Oxygen''s valence electrons are in a 3p subshell."]', 0, 'Nitrogen is 2s² 2p³, with one electron in each 2p orbital: a stable half-filled subshell. Oxygen is 2s² 2p⁴, a less stable arrangement, so one of its electrons is easier to remove.', '{"1": "Both are period 2 elements with two shells.", "2": "Oxygen is the smaller atom, since radius decreases across a period. Size would favour a higher value for oxygen.", "3": "Oxygen''s valence electrons are in 2s and 2p, like nitrogen''s."}'),
  ('CHE-0030', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Ionization energy: trends', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 9 (§1.7.3)', 'Potassium (Z = 19) has a lower first ionization energy than sodium (Z = 11). The main reason is that potassium', '["has a smaller nuclear charge than sodium.", "has a larger atom and more shielding from inner electrons.", "has more valence electrons than sodium.", "has a half-filled valence subshell."]', 1, 'Down Group 1 ionization energy decreases (Li > Na > K > Rb > Cs) because atomic size and shielding increase, so the outer electron is held less tightly.', '{"0": "Potassium''s nuclear charge (+19) is larger than sodium''s (+11). Size and shielding outweigh it.", "2": "Both are Group 1 metals with one valence electron.", "3": "Both have a single s electron (3s¹ and 4s¹). This is the same for both, so it cannot explain the difference."}'),
  ('CHE-0031', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Ionization energy: trends', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 9 (§1.7.3)', 'Across a period from left to right, first ionization energy generally increases because', '["the number of shells increases.", "shielding by inner electrons increases sharply.", "the number of shells stays the same while effective nuclear charge increases.", "atomic radius increases."]', 2, 'Along a period no new shell is added but the effective nuclear charge increases, so the electrons are held more tightly and are harder to remove.', '{"0": "The number of shells stays the same across a period. It increases down a group.", "1": "The inner shells are the same across a period. More shielding would lower ionization energy anyway.", "3": "Atomic radius decreases across a period. A larger radius would lower ionization energy."}'),
  ('CHE-0032', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Electron affinity: definition', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 10 (§1.7.4)', 'Cl(g) + e⁻ → Cl⁻(g) has ΔH = −348.8 kJ mol⁻¹. The negative sign shows that', '["energy is absorbed when the electron is added.", "energy is released when the electron is added.", "chlorine loses an electron.", "the reaction cannot happen in the gas phase."]', 1, 'First electron affinity is the enthalpy change when one mole of electrons is added to one mole of gaseous atoms. For chlorine energy is released, so the value has a negative sign.', '{"0": "Absorbed energy would give a positive ΔH, as for oxygen''s second electron affinity.", "2": "The equation shows chlorine gaining an electron, not losing one.", "3": "Electron affinity is defined for gaseous atoms, so the change does happen in the gas phase."}'),
  ('CHE-0033', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Electron affinity: second electron affinity', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 10 (§1.7.4); Quick Check 1.5, p. 11', 'The first electron affinity of oxygen is −141 kJ mol⁻¹, but the second is +844 kJ mol⁻¹. The second value is positive because', '["the O⁻ ion repels the incoming electron, so energy must be supplied.", "oxygen has a half-filled 2p subshell.", "the second electron enters a new shell.", "the O⁻ ion has a larger nuclear charge than the O atom."]', 0, 'Adding an electron to an ion that is already negative means working against repulsion, so energy is absorbed and the second electron affinity is positive.', '{"1": "Oxygen is 2p⁴, not half-filled. Nitrogen is the half-filled one.", "2": "The second electron enters the same 2p subshell (2p⁵ becomes 2p⁶).", "3": "O and O⁻ have the same nuclear charge, +8."}'),
  ('CHE-0034', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Electron affinity: halogens', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 11 (§1.7.4, Table 1.1)', 'According to the textbook''s data, which halogen has the most negative first electron affinity?', '["Fluorine", "Bromine", "Chlorine", "Iodine"]', 2, 'Table 1.1 gives F −328, Cl −349, Br −324 and I −295 kJ mol⁻¹, so chlorine releases the most energy. The book gives the halogen order as At < I < Br < F < Cl.', '{"0": "Fluorine''s value (−328) is less negative than chlorine''s, even though fluorine is smaller. Its small, crowded 2p shell repels the incoming electron.", "1": "Bromine''s value (−324) is less negative than chlorine''s.", "3": "Iodine''s value (−295) is less negative still, because its atom is larger."}'),
  ('CHE-0035', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Electron affinity: electronic configuration', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, pp. 10 to 11 (§1.7.4)', 'Among C, N, O and F, which atom has an unusually low tendency to accept an extra electron?', '["Carbon", "Nitrogen", "Oxygen", "Fluorine"]', 1, 'Nitrogen (2s² 2p³) has a stable half-filled 2p subshell, so it has very little tendency to accept an extra electron. Its electron affinity is very low; the same is true of phosphorus.', '{"0": "Carbon (2p²) does not have a half-filled subshell, and it gains an electron to reach the half-filled 2p³.", "2": "Oxygen''s first electron affinity is negative (−141 kJ mol⁻¹): it releases energy on gaining one electron.", "3": "Fluorine has a strongly negative electron affinity (−328 kJ mol⁻¹)."}'),
  ('CHE-0036', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Electron affinity: alkali metals', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 11 (§1.7.4, Table 1.1)', 'Using Table 1.1 of the textbook, which alkali metal releases the most energy when its gaseous atoms gain an electron?', '["Caesium", "Potassium", "Sodium", "Lithium"]', 3, 'Table 1.1 lists Li −60, Na −53, K −48, Rb −47 and Cs −46 kJ mol⁻¹. Electron affinity becomes less negative down the group as atomic size increases, so lithium has the most negative value.', '{"0": "Caesium has the least negative value (−46), because it is the largest atom.", "1": "Potassium''s value (−48) is less negative than lithium''s.", "2": "Sodium''s value (−53) is less negative than lithium''s."}'),
  ('CHE-0037', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Electron affinity: trends', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 11 (§1.7.4)', 'Moving from left to right along a period, electron affinity generally becomes more negative. Which pair of reasons does the book give?', '["Increasing nuclear charge and decreasing atomic radius", "Increasing shielding and increasing atomic radius", "Increasing number of shells and decreasing nuclear charge", "Decreasing number of valence electrons and increasing radius"]', 0, 'Across a period nuclear charge increases, attracting an added electron more strongly, and atomic radius decreases, bringing it closer to the nucleus. Both make electron affinity more negative.', '{"1": "Across a period radius decreases and the inner shells stay the same. More shielding and a larger radius would make electron affinity less negative.", "2": "The number of shells is constant across a period and nuclear charge increases.", "3": "The number of valence electrons increases across a period and radius decreases."}'),
  ('CHE-0038', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Electronegativity: definition', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 11 (§1.7.5)', 'The power of an atom in a molecule to attract a shared pair of electrons towards itself is called', '["electron affinity.", "ionization energy.", "electronegativity.", "metallic character."]', 2, 'This is the definition of electronegativity.', '{"0": "Electron affinity is the enthalpy change when an electron is added to an isolated gaseous atom, not attraction for a shared pair in a bond.", "1": "Ionization energy is the energy needed to remove an electron from a gaseous atom.", "3": "Metallic character is the tendency of an element to lose electrons."}'),
  ('CHE-0039', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Electronegativity: Pauling scale', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 11 (§1.7.5)', 'The electronegativity values on Pauling''s scale are expressed in', '["kJ mol⁻¹", "electron volts", "picometres", "no units (they are dimensionless)"]', 3, 'Linus Pauling developed a scale of dimensionless electronegativity values, with fluorine highest at 4.0.', '{"0": "kJ mol⁻¹ is used for ionization energy and electron affinity, not Pauling electronegativity.", "1": "Electron volts measure energy. Pauling values have no units.", "2": "Picometres measure atomic and ionic radius."}'),
  ('CHE-0040', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Electronegativity: bond polarity', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 12 (Fig. 1.6)', 'Using the electronegativity values in the textbook (H 2.1, C 2.5, N 3.0, O 3.5, F 4.0), which bond is the most polar?', '["C–H", "N–H", "O–H", "F–H"]', 3, 'Bond polarity increases with the difference in electronegativity. F–H has the largest difference: 4.0 − 2.1 = 1.9.', '{"0": "C–H has a difference of only 2.5 − 2.1 = 0.4, the least polar of these.", "1": "N–H has a difference of 3.0 − 2.1 = 0.9.", "2": "O–H has a difference of 3.5 − 2.1 = 1.4, less than F–H."}'),
  ('CHE-0041', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Electronegativity: trends', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 12 (§1.7.5, Fig. 1.6)', 'The electronegativity of iodine (2.5) is much lower than that of fluorine (4.0) because, down the group,', '["nuclear charge decreases.", "atomic size and shielding increase, so bonded electrons are attracted less.", "the number of valence electrons decreases.", "the atoms become non-metallic."]', 1, 'Electronegativity decreases down a group because added shells increase atomic size and shielding, so the nucleus attracts the shared electrons less strongly.', '{"0": "Nuclear charge increases down a group. Size and shielding outweigh it.", "2": "All halogens have seven valence electrons.", "3": "Elements become more metallic down a group, not less."}'),
  ('CHE-0042', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Metallic character', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 13 (§1.7.6)', 'Metallic character decreases from Na to Cl across period 3 because', '["atomic size increases, so electrons are lost more easily.", "nuclear charge increases and atomic size decreases, so valence electrons are harder to lose.", "the number of shells increases across the period.", "shielding increases sharply across the period."]', 1, 'Metallic character is the tendency to lose electrons. Across a period nuclear charge increases while atomic size decreases, so the valence electrons are held more strongly and are harder to lose.', '{"0": "Atomic size decreases across a period. Easier loss of electrons would mean more metallic character, not less.", "2": "The number of shells stays the same across a period.", "3": "The inner shells are the same across a period. More shielding would increase metallic character."}'),
  ('CHE-0043', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Metallic character', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 13 (§1.7.6)', 'Which Group 1 metal is the most reactive and most electropositive?', '["Lithium", "Sodium", "Potassium", "Caesium"]', 3, 'Metallic character (the ease of losing an electron) increases down a group because size and shielding increase, and this makes the element more reactive. Caesium is far more reactive and electropositive than sodium or lithium.', '{"0": "Lithium is at the top of Group 1, so it is the least reactive alkali metal.", "1": "Sodium is above potassium and caesium, so it loses its electron less easily.", "2": "Potassium is more reactive than sodium, but less than caesium, which is lower in the group."}'),
  ('CHE-0044', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Reactions of Na and Mg: water', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, pp. 13 to 14 (§1.8.1)', 'Magnesium reacts with steam. What are the products?', '["Mg(OH)₂ and H₂", "MgO and O₂", "MgO and H₂", "MgH₂ and O₂"]', 2, 'Magnesium reacts with steam more vigorously than with cold water, giving magnesium oxide and hydrogen: Mg(s) + H₂O(g) → MgO(s) + H₂(g).', '{"0": "Mg(OH)₂ and H₂ form when magnesium reacts slowly with cold water, not steam.", "1": "Hydrogen is released, not oxygen.", "3": "No magnesium hydride forms, and the gas released is hydrogen, not oxygen."}'),
  ('CHE-0045', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Reactions of Na and Mg: oxygen', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 14 (§1.8.2)', 'Sodium metal is stored under kerosene oil in the laboratory in order to', '["prevent it from reacting with air.", "keep it in liquid form.", "stop it from dissolving.", "increase its reactivity."]', 0, 'Sodium reacts vigorously with oxygen in the air, so it is kept under kerosene oil to prevent this reaction.', '{"1": "Sodium is a solid at room temperature; kerosene does not keep it liquid.", "2": "Sodium does not dissolve in air. The oil is there to keep air (and moisture) away.", "3": "Kerosene protects sodium; it does not make it more reactive."}'),
  ('CHE-0046', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Reactions of Na and Mg: oxygen', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 14 (§1.8.2); p. 13', 'Which pair correctly matches each metal with the flame seen when it burns in oxygen?', '["Sodium: intense white; magnesium: golden yellow", "Sodium: golden yellow; magnesium: intense white", "Sodium: lilac; magnesium: brick red", "Sodium: blue; magnesium: green"]', 1, 'Sodium burns in oxygen with a golden-yellow flame. Magnesium burns with an intense white flame, which is why it is used in fireworks and S.O.S. flares.', '{"0": "The colours are the wrong way round.", "2": "Lilac is the flame colour of potassium and brick red that of calcium.", "3": "Neither sodium nor magnesium burns with a blue or green flame."}'),
  ('CHE-0047', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Reactions of Na and Mg: oxygen', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 14 (§1.8.2)', 'When sodium burns in a limited supply of oxygen, the main product is', '["Na₂O₂", "NaOH", "NaO₂", "Na₂O"]', 3, 'With limited oxygen (or at high temperature), sodium forms sodium oxide: 4Na + O₂ → 2Na₂O.', '{"0": "Sodium peroxide, Na₂O₂, forms when sodium reacts with plenty of oxygen in open air.", "1": "NaOH forms when sodium reacts with water, not oxygen.", "2": "NaO₂ (a superoxide) is not the product described for sodium in limited oxygen."}'),
  ('CHE-0048', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Bonding in period 3 oxides', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 14 (§1.9)', 'Which oxide exists as simple covalent molecules held together by weak intermolecular forces?', '["Na₂O", "MgO", "CaO", "SO₂"]', 3, 'Oxides of non-metals such as sulfur are covalent and exist as separate molecules with weak intermolecular forces. The change from ionic to covalent oxides follows the increasing electronegativity of the element.', '{"0": "Na₂O is ionic and exists as a giant ionic lattice with strong electrostatic forces.", "1": "MgO is an ionic metal oxide with a giant ionic lattice.", "2": "CaO is an ionic metal oxide with a giant ionic lattice."}'),
  ('CHE-0049', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Classification of oxides', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 15 (§1.9.1)', 'Which oxide dissolves in water to give an alkaline solution?', '["Na₂O", "SO₃", "P₂O₅", "SO₂"]', 0, 'Na₂O is a basic oxide of a metal. It reacts with water to give an alkali: Na₂O + H₂O → 2NaOH.', '{"1": "SO₃ is an acidic oxide of a non-metal; it gives an acid with water.", "2": "P₂O₅ is an acidic oxide of a non-metal; it gives an acid with water.", "3": "SO₂ is an acidic oxide; with water it gives H₂SO₃."}'),
  ('CHE-0050', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Classification of oxides: acidic oxides', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 15 (§1.9.1)', 'Sulfur dioxide dissolves in water to form', '["H₂SO₄", "H₂S", "H₂SO₃", "H₂S₂O₇"]', 2, 'SO₂ is an acidic oxide: SO₂ + H₂O → H₂SO₃ (sulfurous acid). Sulfur stays in the +4 oxidation state.', '{"0": "H₂SO₄ has sulfur in the +6 state. It comes from SO₃, not SO₂.", "1": "H₂S contains no oxygen; it is not formed when an oxide dissolves in water.", "3": "H₂S₂O₇ (oleum) forms from SO₃ and sulfuric acid, not from SO₂ and water."}'),
  ('CHE-0051', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Classification of oxides: amphoteric oxides', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 15 (§1.9.1)', 'Aluminium oxide reacts with hydrochloric acid and also with sodium hydroxide solution. What is the aluminium-containing product with NaOH, and what type of oxide is Al₂O₃?', '["AlCl₃; basic oxide", "NaAlO₂ (sodium aluminate); amphoteric oxide", "Al(OH)₃; acidic oxide", "Na₂SiO₃; amphoteric oxide"]', 1, 'Al₂O₃ reacts with acids (Al₂O₃ + 6HCl → 2AlCl₃ + 3H₂O) and with bases (Al₂O₃ + 2NaOH → 2NaAlO₂ + H₂O). An oxide that reacts with both is amphoteric.', '{"0": "AlCl₃ is the product with HCl, not NaOH. And reacting with a base as well means it is not simply basic.", "2": "The product with NaOH is sodium aluminate, and an oxide that also reacts with acids is not simply acidic.", "3": "Na₂SiO₃ is formed from SiO₂ and NaOH; it contains no aluminium."}'),
  ('CHE-0052', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Classification of oxides: acidic oxides', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 15 (§1.9.1)', 'Silicon dioxide reacts with sodium hydroxide solution. This reaction shows that SiO₂ is', '["an acidic oxide, forming Na₂SiO₃ and water.", "a basic oxide, forming Si(OH)₄.", "a neutral oxide that does not react.", "an amphoteric oxide, forming SiCl₄."]', 0, 'SiO₂ is an acidic oxide because it reacts with bases: SiO₂ + 2NaOH → Na₂SiO₃ + H₂O.', '{"1": "A basic oxide reacts with acids, not bases. The product with NaOH is sodium silicate.", "2": "SiO₂ does react with NaOH, so it is not neutral.", "3": "No chloride forms in a reaction with NaOH, and the book classes SiO₂ as acidic."}'),
  ('CHE-0053', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Classification of chlorides', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 16 (§1.9.2)', 'Which chloride gives the most acidic solution when added to water?', '["NaCl", "KCl", "MgCl₂", "SiCl₄"]', 3, 'SiCl₄ is hydrolysed by water: SiCl₄ + 2H₂O → SiO₂ + 4HCl. The HCl formed makes the solution strongly acidic.', '{"0": "NaCl simply dissolves (hydration) and gives a neutral solution, pH 7.", "1": "KCl is a Group 1 chloride; like NaCl, it gives a neutral solution.", "2": "MgCl₂ gives a nearly neutral solution, pH about 6.5."}'),
  ('CHE-0054', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Classification of chlorides: hydrolysis', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 16 (§1.9.2)', 'Phosphorus trichloride reacts with water. Besides HCl, the other product is', '["H₃PO₄", "H₃PO₃", "P₂O₅", "PH₃"]', 1, 'PCl₃ is an acidic chloride and is hydrolysed: PCl₃ + 3H₂O → H₃PO₃ + 3HCl. Phosphorus keeps its +3 oxidation state, so the acid is H₃PO₃ (phosphorous acid).', '{"0": "H₃PO₄ has phosphorus in the +5 state. PCl₃ has phosphorus in the +3 state.", "2": "P₂O₅ is an oxide, not a product of hydrolysis in water.", "3": "PH₃ (phosphine) would need phosphorus to be reduced to −3; it is not formed."}'),
  ('CHE-0055', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Classification of chlorides: hydration', 'easy', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 16 (§1.9.2)', 'When NaCl dissolves in water, its ions become surrounded by water molecules without any reaction with water. This process is called', '["hydrolysis.", "neutralisation.", "hydration.", "dehydration."]', 2, 'Ions surrounded by water molecules are hydrated ions, and the process is hydration. The solution stays neutral.', '{"0": "Hydrolysis is a reaction with water, as shown by chlorides such as AlCl₃, which makes the solution acidic.", "1": "Neutralisation is the reaction of an acid with a base.", "3": "Dehydration is the removal of water, the opposite process."}'),
  ('CHE-0056', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Oxidation numbers in oxides', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 17 (§1.10, Table 1.2)', 'What is the oxidation number of phosphorus in P₄O₆?', '["+6", "+5", "+4", "+3"]', 3, 'Oxygen is −2, so 4x + 6(−2) = 0, giving x = +3.', '{"0": "+6 would need 4 × 6 = 24 positive charges to balance only 12 negative ones.", "1": "+5 is the oxidation number of phosphorus in P₄O₁₀, not P₄O₆.", "2": "+4 would need 16 positive charges to balance 12 negative ones."}'),
  ('CHE-0057', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Oxidation numbers in chlorides', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 17 (§1.10, Table 1.2)', 'Among these period 3 chlorides, in which does the period 3 element have the highest oxidation number?', '["AlCl₃", "SiCl₄", "PCl₅", "SCl₂"]', 2, 'Table 1.2 gives phosphorus +5 in PCl₅, the highest of these. In chlorides the oxidation number rises from +1 (Na) to +5 (P).', '{"0": "Aluminium is +3 in AlCl₃.", "1": "Silicon is +4 in SiCl₄.", "3": "Sulfur is only +2 in SCl₂."}'),
  ('CHE-0058', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Variable oxidation states', 'hard', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, p. 17 (§1.10)', 'Sulfur shows both +4 (in SO₂) and +6 (in SO₃). This variable oxidation state is possible because sulfur', '["has a half-filled 3p subshell.", "can expand its octet by promoting electrons into empty 3d orbitals.", "is more electronegative than oxygen.", "loses electrons from its inner shell."]', 1, 'Phosphorus and sulfur can expand their octet by exciting electrons into empty 3d orbitals, so they can use different numbers of valence electrons for bonding: four in SO₂ (+4) and all six in SO₃ (+6).', '{"0": "Sulfur is 3s² 3p⁴, which is not half-filled.", "2": "Sulfur is less electronegative than oxygen, which is why its oxidation number in its oxides is positive.", "3": "Only valence electrons are used in bonding; inner-shell electrons are not involved."}'),
  ('CHE-0059', 'Periodic table and periodicity', 'Oxidation numbers in oxides and chlorides', 'moderate', 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 Periodic Table and Periodic Properties, pp. 16 to 17 (§1.10)', 'In the oxides and chlorides of period 3 elements, the period 3 element always has a positive oxidation number because', '["oxygen and chlorine are more electronegative than any of these elements.", "period 3 elements are all metals.", "period 3 elements always lose all their valence electrons.", "oxygen and chlorine are less electronegative than these elements."]', 0, 'The oxidation number corresponds to the electrons used in bonding. It is always positive because oxygen and chlorine are more electronegative than any of these elements.', '{"1": "Period 3 also contains non-metals such as phosphorus, sulfur and chlorine.", "2": "They do not always use all their valence electrons: sulfur is +4 in SO₂ and phosphorus +3 in PCl₃.", "3": "It is the other way round. If oxygen and chlorine were less electronegative, the period 3 element would be negative."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

-- New Chemistry questions written in the admin panel continue from CHE-0060.
INSERT INTO question_counters (prefix, n) VALUES ('CHE', 59) ON CONFLICT (prefix) DO UPDATE SET n = max(n, 59);

-- The same questions in the other banks.
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO bank_questions (bank, question_id, subject, chapter)
SELECT b.column1, q.id, 'Chemistry', b.column2
FROM questions q
JOIN (VALUES ('fsc1', 'Periodic table and periodic properties'), ('nums', 'Periodic table and periodicity'), ('aku', 'Periodic table and periodicity')) AS b
WHERE q.id BETWEEN 'CHE-0001' AND 'CHE-0059' AND q.source LIKE 'Chemistry 11 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 1 %';
